You are tasked with creating a simple banking system in Python. Refer to the following class diagram to know which classes, properties, and methods to create.
3 classes, showing Bank, Account, and Savings Account, with their relationship arrows, properties, and methods.
The Account class will serve as a base (parent) class and contains a private balance property. It has functions to get the current balance, deposit money, and withdraw. This class has no inputs and few print functions.
The constructor function accepts the account name and account number and sets them.
The deposit function accepts a number and increases the balance property.
The withdraw function accepts a number to deduct from the balance. If the amount is greater than balance, it should show a message like “Insufficient funds”. Otherwise, it can take from balance.
The __str__ function returns a formatted string that displays the account name, account number in brackets [ ], and the balance preceded by a P. It would look like “JMRI [1234] P 1000”. This is not a print(), it uses a return.
The destructor function just acknowledges that the account is closed. It prints “Account <number> closed” once the account is destroyed.
The SavingsAccount class serves as a child class to the Account. It can do everything account can with an added “addInterest” function that lets an account earn interest when the bank calls it. There is also a private attribute named “interest” which sets the interest rate that savings account can earn in interest.
The Bank class is modeled after a commercial bank that keeps a collection of accounts. It has a private list of accounts and a name which gets set when a bank is instantiated (created). This class likewise handles message outputs and inputs of data inside each function.
The constructor function accepts the name of the bank.
The showAccounts function will print each account details in the accounts list.
The openAccount function creates a new account. It asks for user input for the name, number, and type of account (savings or checking).
The closeAccount function deletes the account, which calls its destructor function.
The addInterest function adds interest to the balance of all savings accounts.
The destructor function thanks the user for banking with them and subsequently closes all accounts.
The deposit function asks the user for the account to deposit to using its account number, then deposits the amount using the account’s own deposit function.
